What is taken from chardetng 1.0.0:
  - single_byte.mbt: the single-byte candidates (LatinCandidate,
    NonLatinCasedCandidate, ArabicFrenchCandidate, CaselessCandidate,
    LogicalCandidate, VisualCandidate) and their constants, ported from
    src/lib.rs, and SingleByteData with its classification and pair scoring,
    ported from src/data.rs.
  - chinese.mbt: the GBK and Big5 candidates (GbkCandidate, Big5Candidate)
    and their constants, ported from src/lib.rs. chardetng starts a new GBK
    decoder after two kinds of malformed bytes; the port leaves that out
    because its decoder starts over after any malformed sequence.
  - japanese.mbt: the Shift_JIS, EUC-JP and ISO-2022-JP candidates
    (ShiftJisCandidate, EucJpCandidate, Iso2022Candidate) and their
    constants, ported from src/lib.rs.
  - korean.mbt: the EUC-KR candidate (EucKrCandidate) and its constants,
    ported from src/lib.rs.
  - detector.mbt: how EncodingDetector feeds candidates (skipping leading
    ASCII, keeping two bytes of context, treating the end of the stream as a
    space), how Candidate::score adjusts a score for a top-level domain,
    find_score, guess and tld_may_affect_guess, ported from src/lib.rs.
  - tld.mbt: the classes of top-level domains, classify_tld and
    contains_upper_case_period_or_non_ascii, ported from src/tld.rs, and
    encoding_for_tld, encoding_is_native_to_tld and score_adjustment, ported
    from src/lib.rs.
  - detector_data.mbt: the byte classification and character-pair score
    tables and the frequent simplified Hanzi, Kanji and Hangul from
    src/data.rs, and the top-level domain tables from src/tld.rs, written by
    tools/gen_data.py without changes. src/data.rs and src/tld.rs both
    state: "Any copyright is dedicated to the Public Domain.
    https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
  - The expected scores and guesses in golden_data_test.mbt, produced by
    running chardetng 1.0.0 (tools/golden).
  - The unit tests of chardetng's src/lib.rs. chardetng_tests_data_test.mbt
    holds the name, the expected encoding and the text of each test that is
    one call of `check`, with the text encoded as `check` encodes it,
    written by tools/golden with encoding_rs 0.8.42 and detone 1.0.0;
    chardetng_tests_test.mbt ports the other tests, their names, bytes and
    expected encodings.

encoding_rs 0.8.42
  https://github.com/hsivonen/encoding_rs
  encoding_rs is copyright Mozilla Foundation, licensed under the Apache
  License, Version 2.0 or the MIT license, at your option.

  cjk_decoder.mbt, japanese_decoder.mbt and korean_decoder.mbt decode
  GB18030, Big5, Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, ISO-2022-JP and EUC-KR as the Encoding
  Standard specifies; how they report
  a malformed sequence byte by byte (its length, and whether a following
  ASCII byte belongs to it) follows the decoders of encoding_rs 0.8.42,
  which chardetng's candidates are fed with. No code is copied.
  golden_trace_wbtest.mbt holds hashes of how the encoding_rs decoders
  answer, and golden_labels_test.mbt the encoding that Encoding::for_label
  of encoding_rs finds for each input, both produced by tools/golden.

  decode.mbt reports the first malformed sequence of an input where
  decode_to_string_without_replacement of encoding_rs 0.8.42 reports it,
  including which of two ISO-2022-JP escape sequences in a row is in error,
  and removes a byte order mark as its new_decoder_with_bom_removal does.
  No code is copied. decode_data_test.mbt holds hashes of what the
  encoding_rs decoders give, produced by tools/golden.

